TI  - Brillouin Scattering by Gases as a Test of the Boltzmann Equation
JO  - The Physics of Fluids
PY  - 1966/02
VL  - 9
IS  - 2
SP  - 380-381
PB  - AIP Publishing
DO  - 10.1063/1.1761682
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1761682
AB  - The frequency distribution of monochromatic light scattered by a gas is proposed as an experimental test of kinetic theory away from the hydrodynamic regime. The phenomenon is closely analogous to high-frequency sound propagation and is governed by the same linearized Boltzmann equation. The problem can be posed as an initial value problem in an infinite medium. The exact numerical solutions for Maxwell molecules of the preceding paper give an estimate of the results to be expected.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
AU  - Yip, Sidney
TI  - Rayleigh Scattering in Dilute Gases
JO  - The Journal of the Acoustical Society of America
PY  - 1971/03
VL  - 49
IS  - 3C
SP  - 941-949
PB  - Acoustical Society of America (ASA)
DO  - 10.1121/1.1912453
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1121/1.1912453
AB  - Large-angle Rayleigh scattering of light provides a tool for studying fluctuations in dilute gases where the wavelength is comparable to the mean free path. Interpretation of such an experiment requires a molecular calculation of the spectrum of the density fluctuation. The initial value problem can be formulated using the linearized Boltzmann equation. Solutions are obtained for two types of repulsive intermolecular potential, Maxwell molecule and rigid sphere, and the results are used in a quantitative analysis of high-resolution measurement. The problem of studying molecular gases in a similar way is also discussed.
